# Design Context — microapps

## Target audience

Global English speakers skewing young (20s–30s), heavily Southeast Asia (Thailand,
Vietnam, Indonesia, Philippines) plus US/UK. Many are non-native English readers, so
prose is short and plain. **Mobile-first: 390px is the primary design width** — most
visitors arrive from a link on X, on a phone.

## Use cases

Micro-payment consumer apps around Chinese astrology (사주 / BaZi) and adjacent
fortune-telling formats. Each app: a 15-second input, a free result the user wants to
screenshot and post, and a $0.99 paywall over the detailed reading.

**The free result screen is the distribution engine.** It must look finished and
postable on its own. The paywall sells *why* that result, never the result itself.

## Brand personality / tone

Assertive, never hedging. Second person plural. Short sentences. The reading should feel
like it knows something. Nothing may look like an ad or a chrome-heavy SaaS dashboard —
the page should read as **an object, not an app**.

## Red Thread — the established design system

Approved 2026-09-02. Reference implementation: `docs/design/red-thread-mockup.html`
(self-contained, opens from disk). Treat it as the source of truth for presentation.

**One layout, two materials.** Light and dark are not two designs — one DOM, one layout,
one type scale, one spacing rhythm, rendered in two materials. Every element exists in
both modes in the same place.

- **Light — 먹과 주사 (Ink & Vermilion):** warm paper ground, ink that is never pure
  black, vermilion as the single accent used in very few places. Letterpress.
- **Dark — 자정의 의식 (Midnight Ritual):** stacked near-black navies for depth, gold as
  a material with facets and grain. **Never a glow, never a backdrop-filter.**

**Fonts (one set, both modes):** Iowan Old Style (display) / Charter (body) /
Optima (labels) / Hiragino Mincho ProN (CJK glyphs).

**Two signature elements, both load-bearing:**
1. **The thread** — one continuous, traceable red line that hooks through the date
   inputs, passes through the counters of the score's digits like a needle's eye, and
   ties off in a knot. This is the brand.
2. **The ecliptic chart** — the real 12-branch chart with the animals' trine drawn, lit
   vertices for the pair and an unlit dashed vertex for the absent one.

**Withheld (paid) text is never blurred.** Light: letterpress misregistration plus an
off-square 秘 stamp. Dark: unlit low-contrast text behind gold louvers plus a wax seal.
**Section titles always stay perfectly legible** — they are the appetite.

**Theme mechanics:** complete light palette on bare `:root`; dark deltas under
`@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` guarded as `:root:not([data-theme="light"])`; the
same deltas again under `:root[data-theme="dark"]`. Never define a color only inside a
media or `[data-theme]` block. `--is-dark` (0/1) drives mode-dependent geometry directly
so component rules stay mode-agnostic.

## Hard constraints

- Zero runtime variable cost: no LLM API, no image generation API, no paid external API.
- No login, no accounts. No ads.
- Readings are never stored server-side — the URL fully determines the result.
- Verify no horizontal overflow at 390, 430, 768, 1280.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. Pages must be correct with JS disabled.
- `<input type="date">` prints its digits in the browser's UI locale and no web API can
  override that. The fix is a static English echo under the field, not a fake input.

## The bar

The AI-slop test governs: no purple-blue gradients, no glassmorphism, no neon-on-dark,
no nested cards, no identical card grids, no gradient text, no generic drop-shadowed
rounded rectangles. If someone would say "an AI made this," it has failed.
